### 2026-08-15T11:59:59.699Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

**Fixed and committed** (`[REDACTED_SECRET]`). Now, your actual question — what could have prevented this:

**Root cause wasn't in yesterday's feature at all.** `sw.js` used a cache-first strategy: once a browser precached `index.html` on a player's *first-ever* visit, it kept serving that exact snapshot forever — nothing about a later code change invalidates a cache-first entry, only a manual `CACHE_NAME` bump does. That bump had never once happened in this project's history, despite `index.html` and `js/main.js` each being edited 60+ times. Yesterday's dropdown reorg just happened to be the edit that finally hit a file (`index.html`) whose staleness was *visible* — a returning player's browser kept the old markup while the new JS (fetched live, since it was never precached either) expected an element that didn't exist on their page.

**What I could have done differently:**

1. **Tested as a returning user, not just a fresh one.** Every verification I ran — the Playwright suite and my own manual repro scripts — used a brand-new browser profile every time. A fresh profile has no prior cache to be stale *from*, so this class of bug is invisible no matter how thoroughly you test that way. I only found it once you reported the symptom and I deliberately simulated "a browser that visited before."

2. **Noticed the structural gap, not just the immediate fix.** The Playwright suite blocks service workers globally, and the one exception (the `file://` test) explicitly disables the caching path too — so *nothing* in the suite could ever have caught this, for any past or future change. That's now closed: I added a regression test that fakes a stale precache directly and confirmed it fails against the old code before passing against the fix.

3. **The actual fix removes the failure mode itself**, rather than relying on remembering a version bump next time: `sw.js` is now network-first with cache-as-offline-fallback, so a code change reaches online players on their very next visit automatically. I also bumped `CACHE_NAME` once more to immediately self-heal anyone currently stuck on stale content from before this fix.


