### 2026-08-15T23:54:28.005Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Good push — let me think about this at the mechanism level rather than patch-by-patch.

**What already works, structurally:** `getModes(page)` (`[REDACTED_FILENAME]`) reads the mode list live off the DOM (`data-mode` attributes), so INV-1, INV-3's follow-up, INV-9/12, INV-11, and INV-13's reachability check can never drift — they ask the app what modes exist, every run. That's the actual fix pattern, not a one-off.

**What can't fully follow that pattern, and why:** `STATEFUL_MODES`, `COUNTER_ID_FOR_MODE`, `PRIMARY_ELEMENTS`, `SECONDARY_ELEMENTS`, `CONTAINER_ID` are all hand-maintained maps keyed by mode, and none of them can be *auto-generated* from the DOM — each needs a human decision that isn't observable from markup alone: `mutate()` needs a genuine mode-specific UI gesture; `PRIMARY_ELEMENTS`/`SECONDARY_ELEMENTS` need a judgment call about what's primary vs. secondary (I hit this explicitly building the INV-13 coverage test — a naive "every visible control counts" scan would misclassify Compose's own settings inputs). So the content of these maps genuinely can't be automated away.

**But whether every mode has *an entry at all* can be checked automatically** — that's exactly the axis `getModes()` already nails, just never cross-referenced against these maps. The general mechanism: one shared assertion that diffs `getModes()`'s live output against a map's own keys and fails loud, listing exactly which modes are missing an entry. This is the same shape as the INV-13 coverage-nudge test I already built (which checks "every *control* is classified" against `PRIMARY_ELEMENTS`/`SECONDARY_ELEMENTS`) — but that one operates one level down (controls within a known mode); this would be the missing sibling layer (modes themselves within a known map), applied uniformly to every such map instead of writing a bespoke check only after each one gets bitten — which is exactly what's happened twice now: INV-13 got its coverage test, `STATEFUL_MODES` currently has none.

One thing this deliberately does *not* try to do: auto-generate `mutate()` itself by poking internal state. `STATEFUL_MODES`'s own comment is explicit that every `mutate()` must be "a genuine UI interaction... never a direct call into a mode's internal API" — that's a real, intentional design constraint (this project consistently avoids test-only hooks baked into production mode objects, driving everything through real clicks/keypresses instead), and a coverage check should enforce *presence*, not manufacture the content.

Want me to build this — one shared `assertCoversAllModes(page, map, label)` helper, wired into a single new test that checks all five maps at once, so adding a mode without an entry fails loud instead of silently skipping coverage?


